Z||000010||avengador1||11-01-2001||08:02 PM||||Try this link <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/1930/frame.html#links" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/1930/wwfl.html</A>    Its the world wide Fiero links page and this will keep you busy for a while<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000011||Rick 88||11-01-2001||08:03 PM||||LINKS.....I don't need no stinkin LINKS!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Actually, there are more links for Fiero's than just about any other car out there.<BR>Try World Wide Fiero Links to start. That should make you bleary eyed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>Rick 88 ||||reg||10
Z||000012||Oreif||11-01-2001||08:10 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>LT1 350?<BR>Would that completely upset the mid-engine setup???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope, The 350 bolts into the same location as the V-6. If you are interested in V-8 swaps, visit <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A> <BR>He is right here in Barrington, IL and does many V-8 swaps using Chevy V-8's. He can install a 280hp carb'd 350 or if you really want power a built ZZ4 350 with injection that will put you over 400hp.<BR>On his website there is a secion that gives a run down on what is involved with the swap and pics of the swaps. Under the Tech section <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014783.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014783.html</A>  <BR>here he is showing the install of a ZZ4 V-8 into a Fiero with a Ferrari body kit on it.<BR>Archie also sells 2 body kits for Fiero's too.<P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||GTFiero1||11-01-2001||08:12 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.net</A> <P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000014||GTFiero1||11-01-2001||08:23 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/modded_fiero_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||11-01-2001||08:25 PM||||Here's a link to a .zip file with all of my fiero bookmarks so far.  This isn't even close to all of them, but most of the mainstream ones.<P><A HREF="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/fiero_stuff.zip" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Bookmarks</A><P>I agree, the Fiero is addictive.  I got it originally thinking it would be a fun car until I could afford the car I really wanted.  Now I don't care about the other car, I just want to do more stuff to the Fiero.  Once you see how much stuff you can do with/to them, you'll never turn back.  The possibilities are honestly limitless, because of the completely customizable suspension, chassis, and interior.  The only thing to be desired is more storage room, but that wasn't a concern for me.<P>Have fun...this ought to keep you busy for a while    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Old Lar||11-01-2001||08:38 PM||||I drove an MR2 (mid 80's) and liked it. Stock 4 cylider first generation.  But I didn't like the sound.  Handled nice.  I fell for the "fast back" style and bought a new Fiero GT in 87, nice throaty rumble more that adequate accereration with the 5 speed and as able to peg the speedometer (a few times).  I still have that 87 now with about 87K miles, only driven in the summer and now also have an 88 GT 3.4L with a t440 auto.  Set up for highway cruising.  Lots of $ fixing the 88 (bought used).  The 87 has had brakes, clutch, battery,  tires replaced, some paint touch up and headliner.  The car still drives great, but highly maintained.<BR>The Fieros are fun cars to drive, not the fastest on the road and also not the slowest.  There is lots of potential for most any engine swap. Let your wallet be your guide.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Pheagey||11-01-2001||11:33 PM||||   Good looks? The fiero has it: <A HREF="Http://www.geocities.com/lithiumeffects/car.html" TARGET=_blank>Http://www.geocities.com/lithiumeffects/car.html</A> <BR>   (Sorry geocities doesn't allow direct linking).<BR>   As for getting to everything? almost to easy (I've funk'd mine up messing with stuff B4.)<BR>   And so much to be done with them...<P>------------------<BR>"There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<BR>    -Mr.Frosty<P>'88 coupe, 5 speed<P>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html<P>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,||||reg||1
