Z||000025||sloth85GT||11-02-2001||02:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>What makes you guys love your car so much?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Buy one and you'll see. As I was coming into the house today, I turned around and saw my car parked on the street and thought to myself. Damn I love my car!<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000026||pureGSR||11-02-2001||03:21 AM||||Um ... is that a trunk I see?<BR>And the engine bay seems pretty cramp ... is it REALLY hard to work on?||||reg||1
Z||000027||SLYGUY||11-02-2001||03:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>Um ... is that a trunk I see?<BR>And the engine bay seems pretty cramp ... is it REALLY hard to work on?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, they are not as difficult to work on as it may seem. <P>Yes, that is the 'trunk'. I don't use the car to haul much anyways.<P>I had a 86 SEV6 about 8 years ago and never turned back. <BR>They are right by saying that the youngest Fiero you will find is at least 13 years old and you will need to find one that was taken care of. <P>Good Luck!<BR>Sly<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.freakygamers.com/smilies/s/contrib/blackeye/stretch.gif"> <P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||artherd||11-02-2001||05:54 AM||||Come on, do it, get one! You'll love it!<BR>(just make sure you have another car to get around in, Fieros are many things, but 'reliable' in stock form I would not say...)<P>88s are probally the best, but cost the most.<BR>Get one with not less than 50,000miles (you want something that hasn't sat in one place for ten years) be weary of anything over 100k, unless you like working on them.<P>They out-corner any MR2 inciadentally (and onlt the Gen2 Turbo MR2 is faster.  Nice car actually, I wouldn't mind owning one of those.) Other than that, they're really very similar, with the Fiero being a bit bigger and lower all around.   Both are guarenteed mid-engined FUN!<P>Come on, get one get one!<P>It's a Ferrari for Five Grand, what more do you want?!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000029||Oreif||11-02-2001||08:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>Um ... is that a trunk I see?<BR>And the engine bay seems pretty cramp ... is it REALLY hard to work on?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have done all kinds of work on Fiero's (Mine and others) and like any other car there are some difficult tasks. But on average they are easier to work on then most cars. Knowing the "ins and outs" of how to do things is the best way. There is just about everything you want to know about any repair here. We have seen them all. Also this is a great source for "common" problems and how to prevent problems.<P>If your really interested, on the 17th (about 2 weeks from now) the Northern Illinios Fiero Enthusiasts club has a meeting. Why don't you come out and see a few Fiero's up close and talk to some of the members? For more info go to: <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/Events.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/Events.htm</A> <P>Joining a local club will assist you in finding a good car and access to inexpensive parts. Here at Pennock's you have access to a wealth of information on taking care and repairing (if needed) your Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000030||1FST2M6||11-02-2001||08:44 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  i work p/t for the premier dyno tuning and custom fabrication shop in the SE. on anything from the turbo specialtys 380FWHP Turbo Type-R to the bad mass air meter on a 98 Range Rover 4.0 Discovery. I've messed with everything... the B18s aren't really that more open than our 60* V6s... i've been spraying my car for over 3 years with no problems... i get razzed by the Rustang guys untill i smoke one on street tires then they all shut up. the feeling of over 1g acceleration is VERY costly in a FWD car. the MRs are decent but again.. the torque we have is superior and our parts are cheaper and easier to get. better brakes are cheaper, a V8 swap is fairly cheap.. about the cost of a good rebuild and new turbo on a 3SGTE. <P>my web site... <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||Wannabe||11-02-2001||09:54 AM||||It is debatable whether the 2nd gen MR2 handles better than the '88 Fieros. The V6 is not hard to work on, parts are cheap and plentiful, but it really is not a very exciting powerplant. No problem though, there are plenty more outrageously good engines that can be swapped in. including the LT1, Northstar, 3800 SC and updated versions of the V660. BTW, as much as people have complained about the Fiero being heavy, the heaviest Fiero is still lighter that the heaviest MR2. One last thing, not to bash MR2s as they are fine cars, for the price of a mildly worked 2nd gen MR2, you can have a totally outrageous Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000032||DreXteR||11-02-2001||12:37 PM||||Hi  Welcome to the forum.<P>Just a few things what make me buy a Fiero and not an MR2 Turbo.<P>1. Are Nice looking car.<BR>2. The body never rust.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>3. You can make almost anything with it. If have the $$$$.<BR>4. All upgrades are cheaper than any car.<BR>5. Its Mid engine. Love that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>6. Are only fews out there, make it "only for a limited oners". Not every one will have 1   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>7. In a nice shape it will out shine any car. Ask for Skitime pics.<P>Here is a Pic of mine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.centennialpr.net/drexter/Fiero/Final.jpg"> <P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000033||Butter||11-02-2001||03:48 PM||||pureGSR,<BR>The Fiero has something better than an OnStar system, It has PFF!||||reg||14
Z||000034||Kelvin Vivian||11-04-2001||01:15 AM||||Below are pics of my babies... Once you start thinking about getting a Fiero, you fall in love with them.<P>1987 SE 4-cylinder ~100hp, burgundy/grey, factory options: 5-spd Isuzu tranny, spoiler (wing), sunroof, performance sound (subwoofer), manual windows, manual mirrors, manual locks, tilt, AC, trunk release.<P>1988 GT V6 ~140hp, bright red/tan, factory options: 5-spd Getrag tranny, spoiler, T-Tops, factory leather seats, PM, PL, PW, cruise, rear defrost, trunk release, vanity mirror (passenger), tilt, AC<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=no_18&album_id=36084&image_id=0&courtesy=1">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=no_18&album_id=36084&image_id=1&courtesy=1">  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=no_18&album_id=36084&image_id=3&courtesy=1">    <BR>88 GT, 5-spd<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||1
Z||000035||3.8T Fiero GT||11-04-2001||01:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>Hi,<BR>I crashed my car (import) last week and now I'm looking around for a different project car.  I stumbled across this forum, expecting it to be a typical GM board.  But even just after 5 minutes of browsing and reading through some of the posts, you guys seem very different from the typical Firebird drivers.  You guys are actually open-minded and willing to share your knowledge with each other.  <BR>Well, I don't own a Fiero and I don't really know anything about them besides the fact that they are RWD mid-engine cars.  If anyone can give me an overview or direct me to the right direction, that would be awesome.  <BR>Couple questions I have in mind are:<BR>1) how hard exactly is it to work on the engine?<BR>2) how does it compare to the MR2's?<BR>3) what kind of swap can i do?<BR>4) how well does the car handle and how reliable is it?<BR>Thanks in advance!<BR>~Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hey man, well all teh questions u asked seemd to be answerwed above.  i love the 2nd gen. MR2 and was looking to buy but they were too much $$$ and so were parts!<P>I had a 88 civic with a JDM DOHC ZC swap and am into the imports in Toronto. but i wanted sumpting different with AMAZING potential and the Fiero is perfect!  not to mention handling and so on...my buddy has a teg as well.  man, with a done Fiero, u will spend nothign compared to what it takes to do up an import!!!  plus power is EASY since u can throw in a V6 with no trouble at all!  all in a car less than 3000 lbs!  i love imports for what they are worth....but i would only buy HIGH end imports now, as in Supra, RX7, etc...since a Fiero can keep up to htese EASY when done right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>i wanted sumptin different and classic (by classic i mean RWD, mid engine of course) and got a Fiero, u should get an 87 or 88...plus my mechanic is a GM freak!  its setup is a TRUE sports car! sure some ppl mock, but they dont research as they should.  <P>its all up to u in the end man, the way i see it is i would never get the chance to drive this car had i been not born in the era, i am 22 and have the rst of my life to drive everything else!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR><B>"A 3.8T makes and excellent "RICE" cooker"</B>||||reg||14
Z||000036||Boomtastic||11-04-2001||02:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pureGSR:<BR><B>what kind of swap can i do?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What did you have in mind?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/front_12.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wheelie_bars_1.jpg">  <P>There's more pics on the web page - <A HREF="http://www.boomtastic.com" TARGET=_blank>www.boomtastic.com</A> <P>Feel free to stop in and drool .. err, enjoy!<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Boomtastic (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000037||avengador1||11-04-2001||04:30 PM||||The reason I bought my car was to have something a little more rare than a Mustang, Firebird/Camaro. Our cars are attrative, their styling still holds up, cheap to insure, and relatively trouble free once you fix the 13 to 18 year old parts that are bound to fail. They also have a lot of performance potential if you want that. What I really like is that they are few and far between and some people don't even know what they are, only that they like them.||||reg||14
